                    "url": "https://ppimconference.com/call-for-papers/",
                    "title": "2026 CALL FOR PAPERS",
                    "content": "The analyzed data include information about pipe joints and appurtenances provided by ILI data and/or pipeline operator documentation, as well as pipe anomaly characteristics such as: anomaly classification, depth, length, width, relative location to pipe wall, and anomaly origin (e.g., mill, construction, corrosion). Incorporating ILI signal data review in the asset integrity management process helps operators react to potential integrity threats and monitor critical pipeline segments for potential emerging threats. Extreme value analysis (EVA) was applied to estimate the maximum external corrosion depths and the associated failure pressure ratios (FPRs) of remnants, utilizing ILI and excavation data from the inspected portions of pipelines to inform predictions. The data they provide is crucial for monitoring pipe movement, managing geohazards, and broader strain-based integrity management of pipelines, including other threats such as corrosion, cracks, and geometry features. + Increased sensitivity of ILI sensors creates much more data that needs to be aligned to the pipeline centerline and pipeline girth welds to perform corrosion growth, anomaly analysis, and other pipeline integrity management operations and procedures.",
